# Phase 6: .deb build / lintian / install / run / uninstall verification
# Performed on the real system, from this repository.

## Build
$ ./scripts/build-deb.sh
  -> dist/portscope_0.1.0_amd64.deb  (543 KiB, Installed-Size 2252 KiB)

## Package metadata (derived, not guessed)
Depends:      libadwaita-1-0 (>= 1.5), libgtk-4-1 (>= 4.14),
              libc6 (>= 2.39), libgcc-s1 (>= 4.2),
              libglib2.0-0t64 (>= 2.80), libpango-1.0-0
Recommends:   iproute2, systemd

The GTK/libadwaita floors come from the crate feature gates actually compiled
(v4_14 / v1_5); dpkg-shlibdeps alone reports only 4.12.0 / 1.3~alpha, which
would let the package install on a system where it cannot run.
iproute2/systemd are Recommends, not Depends: without them PortScope starts and
reports the missing tool instead of failing to install.

## lintian
$ lintian --tag-display-limit 0 dist/portscope_0.1.0_amd64.deb
W: portscope: initial-upload-closes-no-bugs
  Only remaining warning. Expected for a first upload with no bug number; there
  is no upstream bug tracker to reference yet.
  All E: errors and permission warnings were fixed (see below).

## Reproducibility
Three separate causes were found and fixed; only then did builds match.

1. dpkg-deb stamps ar members with the build time.
   -> SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH is now set by the script.

2. `env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR")` baked the absolute source path into the binary,
   so building the same source from two directories gave different bytes
   (1279288 vs 1278680). `--remap-path-prefix` does NOT affect `env!`.
   -> the development catalogue path is now relative.

3. My own first attempt remapped to `$ROOT`, which made the compiler invocation
   itself differ per checkout. Corrected and documented in the script.

Final check, two independent checkouts, full rebuilds:
  main:  ee6a8042d49d2d515507fd3c7d73f497a1e372b96f3ad5e26f640b457a436631
  clone: ee6a8042d49d2d515507fd3c7d73f497a1e372b96f3ad5e26f640b457a436631
  Byte-identical.

## Install (real system install, not a simulation)
$ sudo dpkg -i dist/portscope_0.1.0_amd64.deb
  Setting up portscope (0.1.0-1) ... ok, no dependency errors.
$ dpkg -s portscope  -> Status: install ok installed

## Registered correctly
$ which portscope portscope-cli
  /usr/bin/portscope  /usr/bin/portscope-cli
$ man -w portscope portscope-cli
  /usr/share/man/man1/portscope.1.gz  /usr/share/man/man1/portscope-cli.1.gz
$ ls /usr/share/applications/io.github.portscope.app.desktop  -> present

## Run the installed binary
$ portscope
  -> launched from /usr/bin, real data: 141 ports / 284 processes / 362 services
  -> permission limits reported honestly (2 ssh.socket endpoints)
  Screenshot: evidence/screenshots/phase6-deb-installed-run.png

## Uninstall
$ sudo dpkg -r portscope
  -> /usr/bin/portscope, desktop entry, icons, man pages all removed
  -> mimeinfo.cache no longer references portscope

## Defects found by this verification (all fixed)
1. umask leak: with umask 0002 the staged tree had 0775 directories and a 0664
   .mo. The script now sets umask 0022 and normalises the tree. lintian
   non-standard-dir-perm / non-standard-file-perm gone.
2. Missing changelog (lintian E: no-changelog). Added data/changelog.Debian.
3. Wrong weekday in the changelog. 2026-10-22 is a Thursday.
4. Missing and then uncompressed man pages (lintian E: no-manual-page,
   E: uncompressed-manual-page). Added and gzipped with -n.
5. Non-reproducible output. Fixed with SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH.

## Compatibility limit that must be stated
libc6 (>= 2.39) is required, from dpkg-shlibdeps on this build.
  Ubuntu 24.04 (2.39) - installs
  Debian 13   (2.41) - installs
  Debian 12   (2.36) - will NOT install
A package for an older libc must be built on that release; a single binary
cannot serve them all.
